The Go rendering: one module whose two architecture files give a run a matrix of
two configurations, because each file's name carries the build constraint of one
architecture and neither names an operating system. The matrix the manifest
declares is what the intersection is read against: a helper is reported only
where it is dead under every configuration it is declared in.

-- fixture.json --
{
  "configurations": ["linux-amd64", "linux-arm64"],
  "symbols": {
    "shared": { "file": "target/main.go", "line": 8 },
    "wide": { "file": "target/main.go", "line": 12 },
    "unusedEverywhere": { "file": "target/main.go", "line": 15 },
    "unusedOnArm64": { "file": "target/boot_arm64.go", "line": 8 }
  }
}
-- target/go.mod --
module example.com/target

go 1.27.0
-- target/main.go --
// Command target exercises the build matrix: a helper both architecture files
// call, a helper one of them alone calls, and a helper neither calls.
package main

func main() {}

// shared is called from the init function of both architecture files.
func shared() {}

// wide is called from the amd64 file alone, so it is live under that
// configuration and dead under the other.
func wide() {}

// unusedEverywhere is called from nothing under either configuration.
func unusedEverywhere() {}
-- target/boot_amd64.go --
package main

func init() {
	shared()
	wide()
}
-- target/boot_arm64.go --
package main

func init() {
	shared()
}

// unusedOnArm64 is declared by the arm64 build alone and called from nothing.
func unusedOnArm64() {}
